{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer and withholding penalties","requirement":"A new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving notices, paying interest and returning it. A landlord who willfully withholds a deposit of a tenant receiving public assistance may be penalized.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"All New Jersey residential tenancies; the penalty provision covers tenants receiving State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential rental dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceedings; unlawful use of deposit money is a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination housing protections, including source of lawful income","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in the rental of housing because of a person's source of lawful income or the other characteristics the LAD protects, and must treat renters equally.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex residences as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All landlord-tenant relationships"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Up to $10,000 for a first offense under the LAD (N.J.S.A. 10:5-14.1a(a)); a discriminating landlord may also have to pay monetary damages and rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential dwelling only with the tenant's consent or through the legal process of eviction, and may not evict or fail to renew a lease without good cause, which must be proved in court.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence in New Jersey; written and oral leases alike.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential dwelling units"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Unconscionable rent increase and notice rule","requirement":"Before evicting for nonpayment of an increased rent, a landlord must serve a valid notice to quit and notice of rent increase, and the increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with any municipal rent control ordinance.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All New Jersey residential tenancies, including municipalities that have no rent control or rent leveling ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential tenancies"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"A rent increase must comply with any municipal rent control ordinance; where none exists a judge decides whether the increase is unconscionable.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the 30-year period runs from completion of construction of the building.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from local rent control for 30 years after construction; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"State exemption overrides local rent control ordinances for newly constructed multiple dwellings.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion cost rent increase ban","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in the converted building, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not prohibited.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Municipal rent control authorization","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law that establishes, governs or controls rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, which locally created boards enforce.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Statewide; rent control exists only where a municipality adopts a rent control or rent leveling ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units in municipalities with rent control ordinances"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding, HUD-subsidized or HUD-insured developments with economic problems, and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Iganamort v. Borough of Fort Lee, 120 N.J. Super. 286 (1973)","quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling. Locally created boards enforce these ordinances.","interaction":"Rent regulation is left to municipalities; the state sets no rents itself.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
